    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic incomplete C2 tetraplegia (G82.52)</content>. Seating review completed and the cushion adjusted; skin intact; no autonomic dysreflexia; PHQ-9 2. [S]<list><item>Continue the current skin, seating and autonomic dysreflexia plans.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Neurogenic bladder with an indwelling catheter (N31.9)</content>. Cystatin C 0.90 mg/L, eGFR 93: stable kidney function. [S]<list><item>Continue catheter care.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Ventilator dependence with diaphragm pacing (Z99.11)</content>. Three hours twice a day on the pacer, his six-month goal reached early. [S]<list><item>Hold at this level through the autumn and review further step-up with Dr. Kovac.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Autonomic dysreflexia: episodes, baseline blood pressure, written plan, and nurse training</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No episodes</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Skin check and Braden scale</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Intact; Braden 12</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seating, cushion, and pressure mapping</td><td>At least yearly and after any change in equipment, weight or skin</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pressure mapping done three weeks ago through a second provider; cushion adjusted for higher left ischial pressure; repeat mapping even</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Catheter care and urinary symptoms</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Kidney function (cystatin C; creatinine unreliable with low muscle mass)</td><td>At baseline, then every 6 to 12 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Cystatin C 0.90 mg/L, eGFR 93; sodium 139, potassium 4.3 mmol/L; creatinine 0.50 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Bowel program</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Effective</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Pacer conditioning, oxygen saturation, and secretion clearance</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pacer three hours twice a day; saturation 97% on the pacer</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Backup ventilator, suction, cough-assist device, and power plan</td><td>Every visit, with monthly checks by his nurses</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Checks up to date; generator serviced</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake, then every 6 to 12 months and with any change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 2</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Venous thromboembolism: leg swelling</td><td>Every visit; prophylaxis only during acute illness, surgery or immobilization</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No leg swelling</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Lengthen time on the diaphragm pacer to at least three hours twice a day by six months, with stable oxygen saturation. In progress; target 2003-10-26 [S]</item><item>No pressure injury: skin intact at every check through six months. In progress; target 2003-10-26 [S]</item><item>Written autonomic dysreflexia plan held by him and every nurse, with all nurses trained, by the two-week visit. Outcome by 2003-04-27: Plan and wallet card in place; all regular nurses trained [S]</item><item>25-hydroxyvitamin D at least 30 ng/mL within three months. Outcome by 2003-06-22: 34 ng/mL at ten weeks [S]</item><item>No unplanned hospital admission through six months. In progress; target 2003-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evaluation and Management of Autonomic Dysreflexia and Other Autonomic Dysfunctions: Preventing the Highs and Lows (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2021); Pressure Ulcer Prevention and Treatment Following Spinal Cord Injury: A Clinical Practice Guideline for Health-Care Providers, Second Edition (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2014); Bladder management for adults with spinal cord injury: a clinical practice guideline for health-care providers (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2006); The AUA/SUFU Guideline on Adult Neurogenic Lower Urinary Tract Dysfunction: Treatment and Follow-up (2021); Management of Neurogenic Bowel Dysfunction in Adults after Spinal Cord Injury: Clinical Practice Guideline for Health Care Providers (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2021); Respiratory management following spinal cord injury: a clinical practice guideline for health-care professionals (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2005); Management of Mental Health Disorders, Substance Use Disorders, and Suicide in Adults with Spinal Cord Injury: Clinical Practice Guideline for Healthcare Providers (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2021); Prevention of Venous Thromboembolism in Individuals with Spinal Cord Injury: Clinical Practice Guidelines for Health Care Providers, 3rd ed. (Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine) (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in three weeks for vaccines. Six-month review in two months. Next scheduled visit 2003-09-14: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

